And one rule for your Google DeepMind preparation:

Never say:

        "I'll use Kafka."

Instead say:

        "I have a long-running asynchronous workload and I don't want it coupled to the user request, s
        o I'll introduce a durable event/queueing layer. Kafka is one implementation option."

Never say:

        "I'll use Redis."

Say:

        "This data is frequently accessed, latency-sensitive, and can tolerate cache misses, so a distributed cache is appropriate."

Never say:

        "I'll use Cassandra because YouTube is huge."

Say:

        "The access pattern is primarily key-based and the dataset is extremely large, 
        so a horizontally scalable distributed datastore is appropriate; the exact choice depends on consistency and query requirements."

That shift—from technology-first thinking to requirement → 
                property → technology—is one of the biggest 
                things that will make your answers sound L5 rather than senior-developer/implementation level.




================================================================================================================================================================
The one sentence to remember for Google L5

If the interviewer asks:
        "Why are you using HLS/DASH?"

Don't say:
        "Because YouTube uses adaptive streaming."

Say:
                "I'm using adaptive bitrate streaming so the client can consume the video as small segments and 
                        dynamically switch between different bitrate representations based on current network and buffer conditions, 
                        allowing us to maintain playback with minimal buffering despite fluctuating bandwidth."

Then, if they ask "Why HLS/DASH specifically?", say:

        "HLS and MPEG-DASH provide standardized ways to describe the available representations through manifests and 
        request the corresponding media segments. The client can then select the appropriate representation as network conditions change."

================================================================================================================================================================
That's the L5-level explanation.

Mental model

Remember just this:

        One huge video → split into small segments → create multiple quality versions → manifest tells player what's available → 
        player chooses the next segment/quality → CDN delivers it.

That's the entire idea behind HLS/DASH + adaptive bitrate streaming.


